    "q-331808": {
      "dom_id": "step-q331808",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider a function $d(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{d(t)\\}=D(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $D(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t=D(s+9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-9.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-331808-0",
          "tag": "custom-select",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "0",
              "dom_id": null,
              "type": "math",
