    "q-332062": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332062",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the left.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
