    "q-332187": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332187",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}$ for $s>5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}\\}+L\\{7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}+7\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-332187-a",
